1
00:00:05,490 --> 00:00:10,650
This is the second part of a two-part module covering an approach to interpretation of chest CT.

2
00:00:12,290 --> 00:00:20,310
Following completion of this module, you should be able to employ a systematic search pattern during interpretation of a chest CT.

3
00:00:22,010 --> 00:00:28,790
Evaluation of the lymph nodes, mediastinum, heart, and pericardium was covered in detail in Module 1.

4
00:00:28,790 --> 00:00:38,270
In this module, we will cover evaluation of the pleura, the lungs, and central airways, as well as the bones and body wall.

5
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:44,760
An example normal dictation with pertinent negatives for these sites is shown here.

6
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:49,420
For example, a normal dictation might read as follows.

7
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:53,080
There is no pleural effusion, mass, or thickening.

8
00:00:53,580 --> 00:00:55,120
The central airways are patent.

9
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:56,980
The lungs are clear.

10
00:00:56,980 --> 00:00:59,700
There are no suspicious pulmonary nodules.

11
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:04,040
There are no aggressive body wall or osseous findings.

12
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:09,580
Remember that the normal pleura is essentially imperceptible on CT,

13
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:15,200
and that the pleural space, that is the space between the visceral and parietal layers of the pleura,

14
00:01:15,980 --> 00:01:19,000
can get filled with fluid, gas, or both.

15
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:26,440
So evaluation of the pleura should include assessment for pleural thickening, mass, or abnormal enhancement,

16
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:30,900
as well as for the presence of pleural effusion, or pneumothorax.

17
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:35,760
A pneumothorax is diagnosed when gas is present in the pleural space.

18
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:43,760
Unless loculated, the gas will collect in the non-dependent pleural space, which is anterior in a supine patient,

19
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:49,300
and there will be displacement of the visceral pleural line, as shown here.

20
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:57,620
Pleural effusions can be secondary to accumulation of simple fluid, that is, a transudate,

21
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:04,440
or complex fluid, termed an exudate of effusion, such as from hemorrhage, malignancy, or infection.

22
00:02:04,440 --> 00:02:11,920
When reporting findings of a pleural effusion, you want to measure the attenuation value of the pleural fluid

23
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:14,260
to determine if it's simple fluid or not.

24
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:20,560
You also want to identify if it is layering as expected for a free-flowing effusion,

25
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:24,900
that is, along the non-dependent portion of the pleural cavity.

26
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:29,760
In a supine patient, that will be along the posterior pleural cavity.

27
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:34,620
Finally, you want to identify if there is any associated pleural mass or thickening.

28
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:38,920
The image on the left is an example of a simple pleural effusion,

29
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:43,680
which is layering as expected along the non-dependent portion of the pleural cavity,

30
00:02:44,260 --> 00:02:48,400
with the meniscus effect along the lateral aspect of the pleural cavity.

31
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:54,340
Contrast that with the complex fluid collection on the right.

32
00:02:54,340 --> 00:02:59,380
In this case, the fluid measures higher than simple fluid attenuation,

33
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:04,440
and there is associated irregular pleural thickening and enhancement.

34
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:10,400
This is an example of an exudative effusion from a malignancy.

35
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:15,620
Here are two examples of pleural effusions which are not free-flowing,

36
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:20,720
that is, they are not layering along the non-dependent pleural cavity.

37
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:25,900
Instead, they are isolated to a fixed pocket within the pleural space.

38
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:27,940
This is termed loculation.

39
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:33,960
The image on the left shows a lentiform collection in the left pleural space,

40
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:36,680
so a loculated left pleural fluid collection.

41
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:43,480
The image on the right shows a left-sided pleural effusion tracking anteriorly and laterally

42
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:50,480
along the non-dependent portion of the thorax in a supine patient consistent with loculation.

43
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:57,300
A hydropneumothorax refers to the presence of both fluid and gas within the pleural space,

44
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:58,540
as shown in this example.

45
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:05,840
The lung windows nicely identify the gas within the non-dependent portion of the pleural space,

46
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:10,620
while the image on the right, shown in soft tissue windows,

47
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:17,640
nicely displays the fluid component in the dependent aspect of the right pleural cavity.

48
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:23,880
Note the gas-fluid level at the interface between the gas and fluid components.

49
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:28,440
And finally, the presence of a right-sided chest strain.

50
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:34,360
Now let's move on to evaluation of the lungs and central airways.

51
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:39,200
A commonly overlooked site on chest CT is the central airways,

52
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:41,720
including the trachea and main stem bronchi.

53
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:46,280
These structures should be assessed for abnormalities in location,

54
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:49,480
that is secondary to displacement from adjacent masses,

55
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:54,000
abnormalities in caliber, including both narrowing and enlargement,

56
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:57,160
and finally for intraluminal masses,

57
00:04:57,820 --> 00:05:02,000
which in most cases actually represent adherent mucus,

58
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:05,100
but in some cases may represent neoplasms.

59
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,840
Shown here are some examples of displacement and intraluminal masses.

60
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:18,500
The image on the left shows a left paratracheal mass,

61
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:22,260
resulting in rightward displacement of the trachea.

62
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:26,920
The remaining two images show examples of intraluminal masses,

63
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:33,920
essentially filling defects within the air column of the trachea on the middle image,

64
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:39,480
and in the right main stem bronchus on the right-sided image.

65
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:46,360
The assessment of the lower airways should include a search for the presence of bronchiectasis,

66
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,680
bronchial wall thickening, and air trapping.

67
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:55,960
Bronchiectasis refers to the irreversible dilatation of the bronchi.

68
00:05:56,180 --> 00:05:58,980
It can be both localized or diffuse.

69
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:02,480
Bronchiectasis has many potential etiologies.

70
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:09,860
CT findings include enlargement of the bronchi such that the diameter of the bronchus

71
00:06:09,860 --> 00:06:13,140
is greater than that of the adjacent pulmonary artery branch.

72
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:18,280
There is a lack of bronchial tapering as you move from central to peripheral,

73
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:25,480
and bronchi within the peripheral one centimeter of the lung often become visible.

74
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:32,580
The image on the left is an axial CT showing diffuse bronchiectasis.

75
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:39,800
The image on the right also shows diffuse bronchiectasis on this coronal CT

76
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:44,000
with associated bronchial wall thickening and mucus plugging,

77
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:47,500
for example, in the left upper lobe, as shown here.

78
00:06:50,930 --> 00:06:56,850
As its name implies, bronchial wall thickening simply refers to the abnormal thickening

79
00:06:56,850 --> 00:07:03,130
of the wall of the bronchus itself, as shown here in these examples.

80
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:08,260
Bronchial wall thickening has multiple causes,

81
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:12,420
but is typically secondary to infection or inflammation.

82
00:07:12,420 --> 00:07:16,760
It may or may not be associated with bronchiectasis.

83
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:23,440
Diseases of the small airways can cause air trapping.

84
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:29,100
Air trapping manifests on imaging as focal areas of hyperlucency,

85
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:30,860
as shown in this example.

86
00:07:31,860 --> 00:07:35,460
These are typically identified on expiratory images.

87
00:07:36,340 --> 00:07:39,880
The patchy nature of the air trapping and hyperlucency

88
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:42,940
actually results in a heterogeneous appearance

89
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:47,700
of the lung parenchyma termed mosaic attenuation.

90
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:51,260
Mosaic attenuation will be discussed in more detail

91
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:53,300
a little later on in this module.

92
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:03,580
This case shows a left-sided pneumothorax

93
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:06,340
with gas collecting in the non-dependent portion

94
00:08:06,340 --> 00:08:09,340
of the left pleural space in this supine patient.

95
00:08:10,180 --> 00:08:13,980
Also note visualization of the displaced visceral pleural line.

96
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:18,200
Evaluation of the lungs should include an assessment

97
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:21,320
of the overall lung volume and attenuation,

98
00:08:21,640 --> 00:08:25,240
including symmetry between the right and left lungs.

99
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:29,580
In addition to overall abnormalities,

100
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:32,160
you need to identify focal abnormalities

101
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:34,700
within the lung parenchyma that are either increased

102
00:08:34,700 --> 00:08:37,680
or decreased in attenuation relative

103
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:39,400
to the normal renal parenchyma.

104
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:43,600
Evaluation of the lung parenchyma should begin

105
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:47,120
with an overall assessment of lung volume,

106
00:08:47,600 --> 00:08:49,020
attenuation, and symmetry.

107
00:08:49,660 --> 00:08:52,720
The image on the left shows the hyperinflated,

108
00:08:53,160 --> 00:08:55,080
lucent lungs typical of emphysema.

109
00:08:55,700 --> 00:08:58,660
The middle image shows an overall increase

110
00:08:58,660 --> 00:09:00,900
in attenuation of the lung parenchyma

111
00:09:00,900 --> 00:09:03,360
secondary to pulmonary fibrosis.

112
00:09:03,940 --> 00:09:08,340
Finally, the right-sided image shows variable lung attenuation

113
00:09:08,340 --> 00:09:12,240
that results in a heterogeneous appearance of the lung parenchyma,

114
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:15,260
often termed mosaic attenuation.

115
00:09:16,360 --> 00:09:18,120
Mosaic attenuation is a finding

116
00:09:18,120 --> 00:09:20,520
with multiple possible etiologies.

117
00:09:22,340 --> 00:09:24,500
Emphysema refers to the abnormal

118
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:26,940
and permanent enlargement of the airspaces

119
00:09:26,940 --> 00:09:28,500
distal to the terminal bronchial,

120
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:31,620
accompanied by the destruction of their walls.

121
00:09:31,620 --> 00:09:36,180
On imaging, this results in low attenuation

122
00:09:36,180 --> 00:09:38,980
of the lung parenchyma with lucent areas

123
00:09:38,980 --> 00:09:40,620
that lack visible walls,

124
00:09:40,780 --> 00:09:44,540
and that will be in contrast to lung parenchymal cysts.

125
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:48,500
And finally, there is a paucity of pulmonary vessels.

126
00:09:51,810 --> 00:09:56,070
Note the hyperinflated lucent lung shown in this image.

127
00:09:56,670 --> 00:09:58,630
In particular, in the circled area,

128
00:09:59,450 --> 00:10:02,510
note that these lucent areas lack visible walls,

129
00:10:02,510 --> 00:10:06,070
and there is a paucity of pulmonary vasculature.

130
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:10,800
Please pause and take a minute to review

131
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:13,460
the three types of emphysema

132
00:10:13,460 --> 00:10:17,340
as classified on the basis of involvement

133
00:10:17,340 --> 00:10:20,060
of the secondary pulmonary lobule.

134
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:23,980
Those three types of emphysema

135
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:26,240
are illustrated on this slide.

136
00:10:26,660 --> 00:10:29,680
The image on the left shows central lobular emphysema.

137
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:32,700
If you look closely within the circled area,

138
00:10:32,700 --> 00:10:36,960
you can see subtle lucencies that lack visible walls.

139
00:10:37,880 --> 00:10:40,580
This is in an upper lung predominant distribution,

140
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:42,940
characteristic of central lobular emphysema.

141
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:48,200
The image in the middle shows paraceptal emphysema

142
00:10:48,200 --> 00:10:51,860
with its characteristic subplural distribution.

143
00:10:52,900 --> 00:10:56,480
And then finally, we have extensive panlobular emphysema,

144
00:10:56,480 --> 00:11:00,520
typically associated with alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency,

145
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:03,580
often with a lower lung predominance.

146
00:11:05,970 --> 00:11:08,210
On the other side of the spectrum

147
00:11:08,210 --> 00:11:10,490
in terms of lung volume and attenuation,

148
00:11:10,970 --> 00:11:12,970
we have fibrosis.

149
00:11:13,510 --> 00:11:15,530
Here are examples of a patient

150
00:11:15,530 --> 00:11:17,930
with end-stage interstitial lung disease

151
00:11:17,930 --> 00:11:19,970
resulting in pulmonary fibrosis.

152
00:11:20,630 --> 00:11:23,410
Here you can see fibrosis is associated

153
00:11:23,410 --> 00:11:25,190
with diminished lung volumes

154
00:11:25,190 --> 00:11:29,570
as well as overall increased parenchymal attenuation.

155
00:11:31,660 --> 00:11:33,040
As previously mentioned,

156
00:11:33,400 --> 00:11:37,460
mosaic attenuation is a finding, not a diagnosis.

157
00:11:38,080 --> 00:11:41,280
There are multiple potential causes of mosaic attenuation.

158
00:11:42,020 --> 00:11:44,180
And mosaic attenuation actually refers

159
00:11:44,180 --> 00:11:46,560
to the variable lung attenuation

160
00:11:46,560 --> 00:11:50,780
resulting in a heterogeneous appearance

161
00:11:50,780 --> 00:11:52,640
of the lung parenchyma.

162
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:57,120
The key is to realize that sometimes the abnormality

163
00:11:57,120 --> 00:12:00,740
is in the areas of increased attenuation,

164
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:03,120
while in other cases the abnormality

165
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:06,760
is represented by the lucent areas.

166
00:12:07,320 --> 00:12:09,240
For example, mosaic attenuation

167
00:12:09,240 --> 00:12:12,600
secondary to air trapping and vascular problems,

168
00:12:13,060 --> 00:12:14,720
including chronic pulmonary embly,

169
00:12:16,980 --> 00:12:20,240
these etiologies result in mosaic attenuation

170
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:23,540
in which the lucent regions are abnormal.

171
00:12:25,830 --> 00:12:28,030
Ground glass opacities, on the other hand,

172
00:12:28,710 --> 00:12:30,630
can result in mosaic attenuation.

173
00:12:31,250 --> 00:12:33,990
In these cases, the opaque regions

174
00:12:33,990 --> 00:12:36,550
represent the regions of pathology.

175
00:12:38,070 --> 00:12:41,330
Moving on to more focal abnormalities

176
00:12:41,330 --> 00:12:42,810
within the lung,

177
00:12:43,730 --> 00:12:46,050
those that will cause increased attenuation

178
00:12:46,050 --> 00:12:48,910
include ground glass and airspace opacities,

179
00:12:49,370 --> 00:12:51,270
as well as interstitial opacities,

180
00:12:51,270 --> 00:12:54,230
atelectasis, as well as nodules and masses.

181
00:12:55,290 --> 00:12:57,050
Here we want to first distinguish

182
00:12:57,050 --> 00:12:59,990
what is meant by a ground glass opacity

183
00:12:59,990 --> 00:13:02,830
versus an airspace opacity.

184
00:13:03,570 --> 00:13:06,290
Ground glass opacity refers to an increase

185
00:13:06,290 --> 00:13:08,330
in lung attenuation

186
00:13:08,330 --> 00:13:12,110
with preserved bronchial and vascular markings.

187
00:13:12,190 --> 00:13:14,590
That is, you can see the pulmonary vasculature

188
00:13:14,590 --> 00:13:17,070
and bronchi through the opacity.

189
00:13:17,070 --> 00:13:21,010
This is illustrated on the image on the left.

190
00:13:21,610 --> 00:13:24,350
Note that you are still able to visualize

191
00:13:24,350 --> 00:13:26,450
the pulmonary vessels and bronchi

192
00:13:26,450 --> 00:13:29,450
through that opacity.

193
00:13:30,250 --> 00:13:32,850
In contrast, airspace disease results

194
00:13:33,730 --> 00:13:37,130
in obscured bronchial and vascular markings

195
00:13:37,130 --> 00:13:39,570
as shown on the image on the right.

196
00:13:40,250 --> 00:13:41,850
Here we have increased attenuation

197
00:13:42,450 --> 00:13:44,010
from airspace disease,

198
00:13:44,010 --> 00:13:46,810
but note that we cannot really make out

199
00:13:46,810 --> 00:13:49,290
the bronchial or vascular markings

200
00:13:49,290 --> 00:13:51,030
within this opacity.

201
00:13:51,790 --> 00:13:55,810
We can identify a more proximal bronchus

202
00:13:55,810 --> 00:13:57,070
that is larger in caliber

203
00:13:57,070 --> 00:13:58,750
that becomes visible secondary

204
00:13:58,750 --> 00:14:03,030
to the adjacent airspace filling process.

205
00:14:03,030 --> 00:14:05,270
This is termed an air bronchogram.

206
00:14:07,470 --> 00:14:09,470
Ground glass and airspace opacities

207
00:14:09,470 --> 00:14:12,150
should be differentiated from atelectasis.

208
00:14:13,230 --> 00:14:15,810
The key imaging finding in atelectasis

209
00:14:15,810 --> 00:14:17,890
is that of volume loss.

210
00:14:18,750 --> 00:14:22,430
There are multiple signs on imaging of volume loss,

211
00:14:22,750 --> 00:14:25,010
including shift of fissures.

212
00:14:25,790 --> 00:14:29,550
In this example, we can see that the left major

213
00:14:29,550 --> 00:14:33,010
or oblique fissure is shifted posteriorly

214
00:14:33,010 --> 00:14:35,450
secondary to left lower lobe collapse.

215
00:14:36,230 --> 00:14:39,050
Also note that you can see air bronchograms

216
00:14:39,050 --> 00:14:40,630
associated with atelectasis,

217
00:14:40,750 --> 00:14:43,090
although air bronchograms are classically

218
00:14:43,090 --> 00:14:45,710
described with airspace disease.

219
00:14:46,710 --> 00:14:49,270
If intravenous contrast is administered,

220
00:14:49,270 --> 00:14:52,590
the collapsed lung parenchyma

221
00:14:52,590 --> 00:14:54,050
should enhance normally.

222
00:14:56,520 --> 00:14:58,260
Interstitial disease can manifest

223
00:14:58,260 --> 00:15:00,660
as thickening of the axial interstitium,

224
00:15:01,280 --> 00:15:03,120
the peripheral interstitium, or both.

225
00:15:04,040 --> 00:15:06,640
This interstitial thickening can be smooth,

226
00:15:06,760 --> 00:15:09,080
which is typical of etiologies

227
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:10,520
such as pulmonary edema,

228
00:15:10,840 --> 00:15:13,340
or nodular, which can be identified

229
00:15:13,340 --> 00:15:16,280
with lymphatic spread of neoplasm,

230
00:15:17,360 --> 00:15:20,000
also called lymphatic carcinomatosis.

231
00:15:20,760 --> 00:15:25,480
This example shows smooth thickening

232
00:15:25,480 --> 00:15:28,460
of the peripheral interstitium characteristic

233
00:15:28,460 --> 00:15:30,060
of pulmonary edema.

234
00:15:32,460 --> 00:15:34,060
Interstitial disease may result

235
00:15:34,060 --> 00:15:35,780
in pulmonary fibrosis,

236
00:15:35,880 --> 00:15:38,020
which, as we have already discussed,

237
00:15:38,120 --> 00:15:40,860
results in overall diminished lung volumes

238
00:15:40,860 --> 00:15:44,320
and increased lung parenchymal attenuation.

239
00:15:45,800 --> 00:15:49,080
Additional findings include interstitial thickening,

240
00:15:49,120 --> 00:15:51,660
in this case subplural in location,

241
00:15:51,800 --> 00:15:56,180
that is, just deep to the visceral pleural surface.

242
00:15:56,180 --> 00:15:59,280
Note that this is a prone image

243
00:15:59,280 --> 00:16:03,680
to help distinguish this subplural interstitial thickening

244
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:07,040
from atelectasis if the patient were supine.

245
00:16:10,530 --> 00:16:14,250
The image on the right shows dilated bronchi

246
00:16:14,250 --> 00:16:17,030
in the region of pulmonary fibrosis.

247
00:16:17,910 --> 00:16:20,310
That's termed traction bronchiectasis.

248
00:16:20,750 --> 00:16:22,290
The bronchi become dilated

249
00:16:22,290 --> 00:16:25,630
because of fibrosis in the adjacent lung.

250
00:16:25,630 --> 00:16:28,710
And then finally, we see honeycombing,

251
00:16:29,250 --> 00:16:30,730
which describes the appearance

252
00:16:30,730 --> 00:16:32,850
of these cystic airspaces

253
00:16:32,850 --> 00:16:35,450
with thick defined walls

254
00:16:35,450 --> 00:16:37,430
that are subplural in location

255
00:16:37,430 --> 00:16:40,030
and tend to occur in multiple layers.

256
00:16:40,130 --> 00:16:41,230
As you can see here,

257
00:16:41,810 --> 00:16:44,710
these little cysts are sort of stacked on each other.

258
00:16:45,230 --> 00:16:48,230
That is in contrast to the appearance

259
00:16:48,230 --> 00:16:50,050
of paraceptal emphysema,

260
00:16:50,330 --> 00:16:52,730
which is also subplural in location,

261
00:16:52,730 --> 00:16:55,270
but occurs as a single layer.

262
00:16:57,010 --> 00:16:58,810
Pulmonary nodules and masses

263
00:16:58,810 --> 00:17:02,610
can also result in focal increased attenuation

264
00:17:02,610 --> 00:17:05,050
within the background normal parenchyma.

265
00:17:05,510 --> 00:17:07,630
The distinction between a lung nodule

266
00:17:07,630 --> 00:17:09,070
and a lung mass

267
00:17:09,070 --> 00:17:11,630
is made simply on the basis of size,

268
00:17:12,050 --> 00:17:17,330
with nodules measuring less than 3 centimeters

269
00:17:17,330 --> 00:17:20,890
and masses measuring over 3 centimeters.

270
00:17:22,490 --> 00:17:25,550
Some sources of focal decreased

271
00:17:25,550 --> 00:17:27,710
lung parenchymal attenuation

272
00:17:27,710 --> 00:17:31,330
include cavities, cysts, and bullae.

273
00:17:32,070 --> 00:17:36,110
The image on the left reflects a pulmonary cavity.

274
00:17:36,110 --> 00:17:39,050
I would think of this as a pulmonary mass or nodule

275
00:17:39,050 --> 00:17:42,190
that underwent central necrosis.

276
00:17:42,190 --> 00:17:44,570
And that liquefied lung parenchyma

277
00:17:44,570 --> 00:17:48,870
gets absorbed by the proximal airways

278
00:17:48,870 --> 00:17:51,230
resulting in a central cavity

279
00:17:51,230 --> 00:17:53,830
that may be gas or fluid filled.

280
00:17:54,370 --> 00:17:56,510
But this cavity will have a thick wall.

281
00:17:57,530 --> 00:17:59,650
That's in contrast to the middle image

282
00:17:59,650 --> 00:18:03,570
which shows a cluster of pulmonary cysts.

283
00:18:03,690 --> 00:18:06,330
Now cyst is a nonspecific term

284
00:18:06,330 --> 00:18:08,770
that describes a thin-walled structure,

285
00:18:08,770 --> 00:18:11,690
so something less than 3 millimeters

286
00:18:11,690 --> 00:18:12,910
in terms of wall thickness.

287
00:18:13,430 --> 00:18:15,050
These tend to be well-defined

288
00:18:15,050 --> 00:18:18,530
and may contain gas or fluid or both

289
00:18:18,530 --> 00:18:22,490
and are larger than 1 centimeter in diameter.

290
00:18:23,310 --> 00:18:26,810
And then finally, a bullae is shown

291
00:18:26,810 --> 00:18:29,610
on the image on the right.

292
00:18:29,850 --> 00:18:32,690
This represents a sharply demarcated area

293
00:18:32,690 --> 00:18:34,990
of emphysema that's larger

294
00:18:34,990 --> 00:18:37,750
or equal to 1 centimeter in diameter

295
00:18:37,750 --> 00:18:41,090
with a thin wall typically defined

296
00:18:41,090 --> 00:18:44,810
as less than 1 millimeter in thickness.

297
00:18:47,950 --> 00:18:49,650
Assessment of pulmonary nodules

298
00:18:49,650 --> 00:18:53,390
should include evaluation of the nodule size,

299
00:18:54,390 --> 00:18:56,130
the margins of the nodule,

300
00:18:56,210 --> 00:18:58,270
whether they're smooth or speculated,

301
00:18:58,830 --> 00:19:01,330
the overall morphology of the nodule,

302
00:19:02,170 --> 00:19:03,770
as well as its composition.

303
00:19:04,430 --> 00:19:07,430
Is there any evidence for calcification or fat,

304
00:19:07,430 --> 00:19:09,870
or could this be a vascular abnormality

305
00:19:09,870 --> 00:19:11,950
like a pulmonary AVM?

306
00:19:13,230 --> 00:19:14,990
If there are multiple nodules,

307
00:19:15,150 --> 00:19:18,270
it is important to assess the number,

308
00:19:18,390 --> 00:19:19,950
that is, single versus multiple,

309
00:19:20,410 --> 00:19:23,070
as well as the distribution in the case

310
00:19:23,070 --> 00:19:24,790
of multiple pulmonary nodules.

311
00:19:26,650 --> 00:19:29,310
Classic imaging features of pulmonary nodules

312
00:19:29,310 --> 00:19:32,770
secondary to hematogenous spread of malignancy

313
00:19:32,770 --> 00:19:34,110
are shown here.

314
00:19:34,890 --> 00:19:36,110
In particular, these nodules

315
00:19:36,110 --> 00:19:40,230
are typically well-defined with smooth margins.

316
00:19:40,830 --> 00:19:42,590
They tend to be multiple in number

317
00:19:42,590 --> 00:19:45,230
and predominate in the lower lung

318
00:19:45,230 --> 00:19:48,850
given that the blood flow is greatest in the lung bases.

319
00:19:50,940 --> 00:19:53,560
Contrast the well-defined smooth margins

320
00:19:53,560 --> 00:19:55,760
of the hematogenous metastasis

321
00:19:55,760 --> 00:19:58,840
with the margins of this nodule.

322
00:19:59,760 --> 00:20:01,620
Note the speculated appearance

323
00:20:01,620 --> 00:20:03,680
at the outer margin of the nodule

324
00:20:03,680 --> 00:20:05,360
at the interface between the nodule

325
00:20:05,360 --> 00:20:06,340
and lung parenchyma,

326
00:20:06,860 --> 00:20:07,580
as shown here.

327
00:20:08,460 --> 00:20:11,020
Speculated margins are suspicious

328
00:20:11,020 --> 00:20:13,940
for primary lung neoplasms.

329
00:20:15,080 --> 00:20:17,140
The composition or makeup

330
00:20:17,140 --> 00:20:19,260
of a pulmonary nodule is important,

331
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,120
especially the presence of fat or calcium,

332
00:20:22,120 --> 00:20:25,580
as this often signifies a benign nodule.

333
00:20:26,360 --> 00:20:28,120
Although the presence of calcium

334
00:20:28,120 --> 00:20:31,180
is often indicative of a benign finding,

335
00:20:31,380 --> 00:20:33,300
not all patterns of calcification,

336
00:20:33,620 --> 00:20:35,540
in fact, are benign.

337
00:20:36,240 --> 00:20:38,100
Benign patterns of calcification

338
00:20:38,100 --> 00:20:41,860
include centrally located calcification,

339
00:20:42,060 --> 00:20:43,880
that is, the calcification is noted

340
00:20:43,880 --> 00:20:47,620
in the center of the nodule,

341
00:20:48,560 --> 00:20:49,760
diffuse calcification,

342
00:20:50,500 --> 00:20:52,020
laminar calcification,

343
00:20:52,180 --> 00:20:54,320
where the calcification occurs in layers,

344
00:20:54,320 --> 00:20:57,020
and, finally, a popcorn appearance

345
00:20:57,020 --> 00:20:58,340
of calcification.

346
00:20:59,320 --> 00:21:03,020
Stippled or eccentric calcification patterns

347
00:21:03,020 --> 00:21:04,260
are indeterminate

348
00:21:04,260 --> 00:21:05,480
and can be identified

349
00:21:05,480 --> 00:21:09,800
in both benign and malignant etiologies.

350
00:21:09,800 --> 00:21:13,540
Please pause the video

351
00:21:13,540 --> 00:21:15,180
and take a minute to assess

352
00:21:15,180 --> 00:21:16,880
both of these pulmonary nodules.

353
00:21:17,880 --> 00:21:20,380
Is the pattern of calcification benign

354
00:21:20,380 --> 00:21:22,780
or indeterminate?

355
00:21:24,470 --> 00:21:27,170
In the example on the image on the left,

356
00:21:27,850 --> 00:21:31,250
note that the nodule shows diffuse calcification,

357
00:21:32,250 --> 00:21:34,690
a finding consistent with a benign etiology,

358
00:21:35,270 --> 00:21:37,030
typically a pulmonary granuloma.

359
00:21:37,030 --> 00:21:42,530
In contrast, the nodule on the right-sided image

360
00:21:42,530 --> 00:21:45,690
shows calcification that is eccentric

361
00:21:45,690 --> 00:21:47,130
within the nodule.

362
00:21:47,930 --> 00:21:51,970
This is an indeterminate pattern of calcification.

363
00:21:54,920 --> 00:21:57,580
Hamartomas are benign neoplasms of the lung.

364
00:21:58,180 --> 00:22:00,800
The key imaging findings to make this diagnosis

365
00:22:00,800 --> 00:22:03,540
are the presence of fat within a nodule,

366
00:22:03,540 --> 00:22:07,580
as shown on the image on the left,

367
00:22:08,520 --> 00:22:11,320
and or the presence of calcification

368
00:22:11,320 --> 00:22:12,420
within the nodule.

369
00:22:12,960 --> 00:22:15,740
The characteristic calcification pattern

370
00:22:15,740 --> 00:22:18,220
associated with hamartomas

371
00:22:18,220 --> 00:22:22,580
is termed a popcorn pattern of calcification

372
00:22:22,580 --> 00:22:27,120
given its resemblance to a popped kernel of corn.

373
00:22:34,900 --> 00:22:36,240
Remember that the distinction

374
00:22:36,240 --> 00:22:38,260
between a pulmonary nodule and mass

375
00:22:38,260 --> 00:22:39,800
is based on size alone,

376
00:22:39,920 --> 00:22:42,380
with nodules being less than or equal to 3 centimeters

377
00:22:42,380 --> 00:22:44,880
and pulmonary masses being greater

378
00:22:44,880 --> 00:22:46,860
than 3 centimeters in size.

379
00:22:48,140 --> 00:22:50,860
In the case of multiple pulmonary nodules,

380
00:22:51,320 --> 00:22:52,660
the distribution is important.

381
00:22:53,940 --> 00:22:57,200
In particular, the distribution of multiple small nodules

382
00:22:57,200 --> 00:23:00,500
can be a key factor in determining their etiology.

383
00:23:01,420 --> 00:23:03,780
Small nodule distribution can be characterized

384
00:23:03,780 --> 00:23:08,060
as centrilobular, perilymphatic, and random.

385
00:23:10,480 --> 00:23:13,500
Centrilobular nodules are located centrally

386
00:23:13,500 --> 00:23:16,960
within the secondary pulmonary lobule.

387
00:23:17,840 --> 00:23:20,580
They tend to spare the pleural surface.

388
00:23:21,640 --> 00:23:23,340
So as you notice on the image on the left,

389
00:23:23,420 --> 00:23:26,300
there are multiple faint ground glass nodules,

390
00:23:26,540 --> 00:23:29,660
but note how those peripheral nodules marked with the arrow

391
00:23:29,660 --> 00:23:33,240
tend to spare the pleural surface.

392
00:23:33,240 --> 00:23:35,420
That is, there's a little bit of visibility

393
00:23:35,420 --> 00:23:39,980
between the nodule and the visceral pleura.

394
00:23:42,150 --> 00:23:44,170
Perilymphatic nodules, on the other hand,

395
00:23:44,230 --> 00:23:47,450
are predominantly located along the pleural surfaces,

396
00:23:47,810 --> 00:23:50,090
including the pulmonary fissures.

397
00:23:50,590 --> 00:23:52,890
They tend to be patchy in distribution.

398
00:23:54,030 --> 00:23:57,170
In this example, we see a patchy distribution

399
00:23:57,170 --> 00:23:58,790
of multiple pulmonary nodules,

400
00:23:59,230 --> 00:24:01,550
which predominate along the pleural surfaces,

401
00:24:02,090 --> 00:24:07,450
most notably the major fissures, as shown here.

402
00:24:10,540 --> 00:24:12,920
Random nodules, as their name implies,

403
00:24:13,520 --> 00:24:14,920
are distributed randomly.

404
00:24:15,100 --> 00:24:17,300
So these tend to be diffuse and uniform,

405
00:24:17,740 --> 00:24:19,440
and because of this random distribution,

406
00:24:19,700 --> 00:24:21,220
can involve pleural surfaces.

407
00:24:22,180 --> 00:24:24,180
So nodules involving the pleural surfaces

408
00:24:24,180 --> 00:24:27,600
may be random or perilymphatic in distribution,

409
00:24:27,600 --> 00:24:31,040
whereas centrilodular nodules tend to spare

410
00:24:31,040 --> 00:24:32,420
the pleural surfaces.

411
00:24:33,600 --> 00:24:37,380
A thorough search pattern for a chest CT

412
00:24:37,380 --> 00:24:40,080
includes evaluation of the body wall

413
00:24:40,080 --> 00:24:43,100
and bones of the thorax.

414
00:24:44,280 --> 00:24:46,340
Osseous abnormalities may be focal,

415
00:24:46,880 --> 00:24:48,180
but can also be diffuse,

416
00:24:48,300 --> 00:24:50,320
such as diffuse metastatic disease

417
00:24:50,320 --> 00:24:55,100
or osseous abnormalities secondary to metabolic disease.

418
00:24:55,100 --> 00:25:01,220
It is also important to evaluate the body wall

419
00:25:01,220 --> 00:25:03,780
for findings such as masses or fluid collections,

420
00:25:04,180 --> 00:25:05,480
as well as gas.

421
00:25:06,800 --> 00:25:09,840
Here are some examples of osseous abnormalities.

422
00:25:10,700 --> 00:25:12,560
The right-sided rib metastasis

423
00:25:12,560 --> 00:25:14,860
with a large soft tissue component

424
00:25:14,860 --> 00:25:18,360
and associated right pleural effusion.

425
00:25:18,360 --> 00:25:23,560
The image on the right shows a more diffuse appearance

426
00:25:23,560 --> 00:25:28,640
of metastasis with multiple focal lucencies

427
00:25:28,640 --> 00:25:31,020
throughout the imaged skeleton.

428
00:25:33,870 --> 00:25:36,590
Here are some examples of chest wall findings,

429
00:25:37,130 --> 00:25:40,830
including a fat attenuation mass

430
00:25:40,830 --> 00:25:42,970
in the right pectoralis major muscle

431
00:25:42,970 --> 00:25:44,950
consistent with a lipoma.

432
00:25:44,950 --> 00:25:49,610
The middle image depicts bilateral breast tissue

433
00:25:49,610 --> 00:25:52,670
in a male patient consistent with gynecomastia.

434
00:25:53,450 --> 00:25:56,030
The image on the right shows trace gas

435
00:25:56,030 --> 00:25:59,090
within the right chest wall soft tissues

436
00:25:59,090 --> 00:26:01,870
that can be seen in association

437
00:26:01,870 --> 00:26:04,770
with a right-sided pneumothorax.

438
00:26:04,830 --> 00:26:06,750
So sometimes chest wall findings

439
00:26:06,750 --> 00:26:08,510
can be important in and of themselves,

440
00:26:08,710 --> 00:26:10,730
but they may also be important

441
00:26:10,730 --> 00:26:12,530
in the fact that they may alert you

442
00:26:12,530 --> 00:26:14,650
to intrathoracic findings

443
00:26:14,650 --> 00:26:18,090
which may otherwise be easily missed.

444
00:26:25,340 --> 00:26:27,080
Please take a minute to review

445
00:26:27,080 --> 00:26:30,380
the patterns of calcification as shown here.

446
00:26:32,550 --> 00:26:34,250
In this module, we have discussed

447
00:26:34,250 --> 00:26:36,070
evaluation of the pleura,

448
00:26:36,790 --> 00:26:38,290
lungs and central airways,

449
00:26:38,370 --> 00:26:41,270
as well as the bones and body wall.

450
00:26:42,230 --> 00:26:44,250
Remember that evaluation of the pleura

451
00:26:44,250 --> 00:26:47,110
should include assessment of the pleura itself,

452
00:26:48,070 --> 00:26:50,210
as well as material which may collect

453
00:26:50,210 --> 00:26:51,230
within the pleura itself.

454
00:26:51,230 --> 00:26:53,230
So the pleura itself may be thickened

455
00:26:53,230 --> 00:26:54,470
or abnormally enhancing,

456
00:26:54,890 --> 00:26:56,570
or there may be a frank mass.

457
00:26:57,730 --> 00:26:58,970
Within the pleura space,

458
00:26:59,090 --> 00:27:01,370
you can have fluid or gas collections.

459
00:27:02,090 --> 00:27:04,370
Fluid collections can either represent

460
00:27:04,370 --> 00:27:06,330
simple transudative effusions

461
00:27:06,330 --> 00:27:09,270
versus complex fluid from exudate

462
00:27:09,270 --> 00:27:11,490
such as infection, hemorrhage, and malignancy.

463
00:27:12,790 --> 00:27:15,010
In terms of the lungs and airways,

464
00:27:15,150 --> 00:27:17,350
we want to begin with an overall assessment

465
00:27:17,350 --> 00:27:20,590
of lung volume and attenuation.

466
00:27:21,370 --> 00:27:24,050
You can also look for focal opacities

467
00:27:24,050 --> 00:27:25,610
within the lungs,

468
00:27:25,770 --> 00:27:27,770
as well as focal lucencies.

469
00:27:29,030 --> 00:27:30,710
You don't want to forget to look

470
00:27:30,710 --> 00:27:31,850
at the central airway,

471
00:27:33,210 --> 00:27:34,490
including the trachea

472
00:27:34,490 --> 00:27:38,170
and right and left mainstem bronchi,

473
00:27:38,570 --> 00:27:40,230
assessing for position,

474
00:27:41,590 --> 00:27:43,310
patency, and intraluminal masses.

475
00:27:43,310 --> 00:27:44,830
And then finally,

476
00:27:44,990 --> 00:27:48,050
you need to assess the bones and body wall.

477
00:27:48,730 --> 00:27:49,970
As previously mentioned,

478
00:27:50,790 --> 00:27:53,710
findings within the bones and body wall

479
00:27:53,710 --> 00:27:55,170
may be intrinsically important

480
00:27:55,170 --> 00:27:56,090
in and of themselves,

481
00:27:56,270 --> 00:27:57,450
or they may alert you

482
00:27:57,450 --> 00:27:59,570
to an important intrathoracic finding

483
00:27:59,570 --> 00:28:01,150
which you may otherwise overlook.

484
00:28:02,330 --> 00:28:04,650
That brings us to the end of this unit.

485
00:28:05,330 --> 00:28:08,030
Please complete the corresponding quiz questions

486
00:28:08,030 --> 00:28:09,790
to receive credit for this course.

